text:
It is said at the beginning: every wise physician fears it. Its fruit is known and its account is well regarded.
If people take it, and experience its essence repeatedly, though it may seem harsh at first, it soon becomes clear and fine.
From it come benefits in the body, subtle in their effect like ambergris, desired for what it contains — when one takes it —
it drives away headache, benefits the brain, and makes the senses sound and clear;
it gladdens the heart, strengthens it, nourishes the soul, so that the heart becomes healthy and vision improves.
Ambergris at its finest is like this, and its resemblance to bal [whale ambergris] is well established.
A great fish in the Sea of Zanj [East African Sea] — its detailed description has been written out.
We now explain its benefits: it fattens the body, fuels lamps with its oil, and lubricates the liver.
It opens the liver, reduces its slipperiness, and has sixty teeth;
Forward on its back are forty things, and below its back are teeth set close together;
In the middle of its jaw are small teeth, and when it bites down it grinds them together;
It is said its frame is like that of animals set together — its flanks are like those of some [other creature], emptied out and hollow;
It is four spans in girth, of great length, and eight spans wide;
Its belly is ugly and misshapen, and between its parts there is a reddish hue on its tongue, and its companion beneath;
If one places oneself upon it and mounts it, it will throw the person off its back and bring harm;
If it does not shake [the rider] off, [the rider] is destroyed. Its strength is such that it carries [things], and its pasture is like musk in fragrance, and its whole body;
It is of such a kind that no animal is able to match it, and among the chain [of creatures] it is continuous...
